WEBVTT

00:00.586 --> 00:02.535
- So we've depicted here the area under

00:02.535 --> 00:05.591
the curve F of X above the X-axis,

00:05.591 --> 00:09.393
between the points X
equals A and X equals B.

00:09.393 --> 00:10.997
And we've denote it as the definite

00:10.997 --> 00:14.765
integral from A to B of F of X, DX.

00:14.765 --> 00:16.474
Now what I wanna do with
this video is introduce

00:16.474 --> 00:21.042
a third value, C, that
is in between A and B.

00:21.042 --> 00:24.140
And it could be equal to A
or it could be equal to B.

00:24.140 --> 00:28.459
So let me just introduce
it, right, just like that.

00:28.459 --> 00:32.042
And I could write that A
is less than or equal to C,

00:32.042 --> 00:34.364
which is less than or equal to B.

00:34.364 --> 00:37.301
And what I wanna think
about is, how does this

00:37.301 --> 00:39.970
definite integral relate to
the definite integral from

00:39.970 --> 00:42.739
A to C and the definite
integral from C to B.

00:42.739 --> 00:44.496
So let's think through that.

00:44.496 --> 00:49.496
So we have the definite
integral from A to C of F of X.

00:50.944 --> 00:53.535
Actually I've already used
that purple color for the

00:53.535 --> 00:55.921
function itself, so we use green.

00:55.921 --> 01:00.921
So we have the integral
from A to C of F of X, DX,

01:02.844 --> 01:05.927
and that of course is
going to, that's going to

01:05.942 --> 01:09.976
represent this area right
over here, from A to C under

01:09.976 --> 01:14.463
the curve F of X, above
the X-axis, so that's that.

01:14.463 --> 01:17.865
And then we can have the integral from

01:17.865 --> 01:22.865
C to B of F of X, DX,
and that of course is

01:24.223 --> 01:28.143
going to represent this
area right over here.

01:28.143 --> 01:30.420
Well the one thing that
probably jumps out at you

01:30.420 --> 01:34.028
is that the entire area from
A to B, this entire area

01:34.028 --> 01:37.423
is just a sum of these two smaller areas.

01:37.423 --> 01:41.909
So this is just equal to
that plus that over there.

01:41.909 --> 01:43.197
And once again, you might say,

01:43.197 --> 01:45.547
"Why is this integration property useful?"

01:45.547 --> 01:49.455
That if I found a C that is in
this interval that's greater

01:49.455 --> 01:52.882
than or equal to A and it's
less than or equal to B, "Why

01:52.882 --> 01:56.723
"is it useful to be able to
break up the integral this way?"

01:56.723 --> 01:58.679
Well as you'll see this is
really useful, it can be

01:58.679 --> 02:01.086
very useful when you're
doing disc, when you're

02:01.086 --> 02:03.684
looking at functions that
have discontinuities,

02:03.684 --> 02:05.451
if they have step
functions you can break up

02:05.451 --> 02:07.789
the larger integral
into smaller integrals.

02:07.789 --> 02:10.097
You'll also see that this
is useful when we prove

02:10.097 --> 02:12.152
the fundamental theorem of Calculus.

02:12.152 --> 02:13.148
So in general, this is actually a

02:13.148 --> 02:14.854
very, very, very useful technique.

02:14.854 --> 02:17.983
Let me actually draw an
integral where it might be

02:17.983 --> 02:22.299
very useful to utilize that property.

02:22.299 --> 02:27.299
So if this is A, this is B,
and let's say the function,

02:28.932 --> 02:31.348
I'm just gonna make it
constant over an interval.

02:31.348 --> 02:34.209
So it's constant from there
to there, and then it's,

02:34.209 --> 02:36.287
and then it drops down
from there to there.

02:36.287 --> 02:38.792
Let's say the function looked like this.

02:38.792 --> 02:42.143
Well you could say that the
larger integral, which would be

02:42.143 --> 02:44.692
the area under the curve,
it would be all of this.

02:44.692 --> 02:45.876
Let's just say it's a gap right

02:45.876 --> 02:48.269
there or it jumps down there.

02:48.269 --> 02:52.165
So this entire area you
can break up into two,

02:52.165 --> 02:55.580
you can break up into two smaller areas.

02:55.580 --> 02:57.836
So you could break that up into that area

02:58.098 --> 03:01.340
right over there, and then this area right

03:01.340 --> 03:04.433
over here using this integral property.
